{:db/id 17592186124674 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186124370 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53776591-13cc-549e-9f32-c5abaeced1dc" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Growth"}] :question/id #uuid "a568fc63-e81c-5210-93f0-bcbd429a9e40" :question/math-academy-id "e-6678" :question/problem "A biologist observed that the growth of the number of fish in a lake is directly proportional to the difference between the lake’s maximum fish population and the current population. Let $P(t)$ be the fish population, measured in number of fish, and let $t \\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum fish population that the lake can support is $200$ fish, which differential equation could be used to model the lake’s fish population? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the fish population $P(t)$ of the lake changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum fish population the lake can support and the current fish population. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d P}{\\textrm d t} = k(a - P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum fish population the lake can support, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a = 200 \\textrm{ fish},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d P}{\\textrm d t} = k(200 - P).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "53776591-13cc-549e-9f32-c5abaeced1dc" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186124766 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53776591-13cc-549e-9f32-c5abaeced1dc" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Growth"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b74483-29bf-585d-92c8-7ccb3f928a45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=kP(70-P)" :db/id 17592186445333} {:answer/id #uuid "7fe52287-4fa4-5c84-ad29-268c6a5296c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(70-P)" :db/id 17592186445348} {:answer/id #uuid "993db06a-cd74-5472-8bca-5491bd9aed54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k{P}^{2}(70-P)" :db/id 17592186445353} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf94966-8385-545a-b21e-358160ff193e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=-k(70+P)" :db/id 17592186445371} {:answer/id #uuid "f700ef32-7ece-558e-a620-e91790aaa0bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=-k(70-P)" :db/id 17592186445372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fe52287-4fa4-5c84-ad29-268c6a5296c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(70-P)" :db/id 17592186445348} :answer-field/id #uuid "d06da1af-5ad6-5a97-9279-9b6036bd2a5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186121193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19a4e47b-fcd5-50aa-804a-b275eeea23d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-106691" :question/problem "A factory's management noted that the growth of the unit production is directly proportional to the difference between the maximum number of units a factory worker can produce and the current production quantity. Let $P(t)$ be the production quantity, measured in units produced, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in days. If the maximum number of units that a worker can produce is $70$ $\\text{units/day}$, which differential equation could be used to model the factory's production? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the production $P(t)$ of the factory changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum number of units a factory worker can produce and the current production quantity. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum number of units a factory worker can produce, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=70\\text{ units/day},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(70-P).\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186125066 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53776591-13cc-549e-9f32-c5abaeced1dc" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Growth"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86d14aa6-7bfa-5d51-a2d3-b51157905d3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dR}{dt} = k(105 - R)" :db/id 17592186122997} {:answer/id #uuid "8deef86d-850d-580f-936a-7b20c647e281" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dR}{dt} = k(1700 - R)" :db/id 17592186122998} {:answer/id #uuid "034daa26-4afc-57bc-9d72-1a947446d502" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dR}{dt} = k(65 - R)" :db/id 17592186122999} {:answer/id #uuid "1f9240f3-5806-5595-840a-e234d5f16554" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dR}{dt} = k(55 - R)" :db/id 17592186123000} {:answer/id #uuid "4f16b14f-06bc-5798-ad2e-b30a874d50ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dR}{dt} = k(85 - R)" :db/id 17592186123001}] :answer-field/id #uuid "be720ede-1dbc-51a2-b8eb-db9c18720fad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186122996}] :question/id #uuid "bdadb029-2e6a-54b7-8c42-d0b37b92a134" :question/math-academy-id "q-37376" :question/problem "A rehabilitation center initially has $20$ children. The number of children in the center $R(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t \\ge 0$ is the time in months. If the maximum capacity of the center is $85$ children, which differential equation could be used to model the population of the rehabilitation center? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality."} {:db/id 17592186308738 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53776591-13cc-549e-9f32-c5abaeced1dc" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Growth"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54f141e1-5d1e-5129-8a5d-1ce524c3a538" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=-k(150-N)" :db/id 17592186305973} {:answer/id #uuid "566d884b-c307-5cb5-900e-d4e916f6e60a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=k{N}^{2}(150-N)" :db/id 17592186305986} {:answer/id #uuid "a8ccb96d-8146-58e1-a658-cc117cb30fef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=-k(150+N)" :db/id 17592186306742} {:answer/id #uuid "ead33e72-291a-5706-b666-4290765ed58c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=k(150-N)" :db/id 17592186307313} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e7bf3e-f305-5b82-8c10-c924a8fa96ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=kN(150-N)" :db/id 17592186307453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ead33e72-291a-5706-b666-4290765ed58c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=k(150-N)" :db/id 17592186307313} :answer-field/id #uuid "fea18a22-4aec-53d2-b22b-ca7ff8019a1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186308336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "54bac72f-651e-55b5-8b4b-fbc4e753ebc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-335135" :question/problem "A wildlife manager noted that the growth of the number of deer in a protected forest is directly proportional to the difference between the forest’s maximum deer population and the current population. Let $N(t)$ be the deer population, measured in number of deer, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum deer population that the forest can support is $150$ deer, which differential equation could be used to model the forest’s deer population? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the deer population $N(t)$ of the forest changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum deer population the forest can support and the current deer population. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-N),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum deer population the forest can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=150\\text{ deer},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=k(150-N).\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186308739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53776591-13cc-549e-9f32-c5abaeced1dc" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Growth"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04aedd39-6cd0-5b6c-a95b-8f1528463bef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(95-T)" :db/id 17592186305194} {:answer/id #uuid "100e6d8a-679a-59a7-9f49-904c4ba960dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(30-T)" :db/id 17592186305298} {:answer/id #uuid "3d8045ca-7194-5335-8e8c-fb093e38eb2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(60-T)" :db/id 17592186305744} {:answer/id #uuid "7c223239-ad05-5364-8640-ceef9053e8b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(100-T)" :db/id 17592186306336} {:answer/id #uuid "eb832421-670f-5c24-a2cd-1bebc2d73fe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(950-T)" :db/id 17592186307318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04aedd39-6cd0-5b6c-a95b-8f1528463bef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(95-T)" :db/id 17592186305194} :answer-field/id #uuid "69e66b97-0745-5e34-a590-bf57a3e5e855" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186307862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5651275c-fd64-5799-85e0-52ab15ebd288" :question/math-academy-id "q-335142" :question/problem "A tutoring center currently has $10$ enrolled students. The number of students in the center $T(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in months. If the maximum capacity of the tutoring center is $95$ students, which differential equation could be used to model the student population of the center? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Since the number of students in the center obeys an inhibited growth model, the rate at which the number of students changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the number of students in the center. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-T),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the tutoring center, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=95$ students, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=k(95-T).\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186308740 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53776591-13cc-549e-9f32-c5abaeced1dc" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Growth"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0be596d2-348f-52d1-bdbb-a8f9262ef683" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dM}{dt}=k(35-M)" :db/id 17592186305252} {:answer/id #uuid "1cc9a611-32b3-5c07-adf4-0f5db91760e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dM}{dt}=k(60-M)" :db/id 17592186305416} {:answer/id #uuid "1fbeaf46-358d-53c4-a292-7cfd16a6e157" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dM}{dt}=k(150-M)" :db/id 17592186305460} {:answer/id #uuid "2b90d980-e2c2-5e82-8176-18a5784c6485" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dM}{dt}=k(250-M)" :db/id 17592186305569} {:answer/id #uuid "6a37b76e-f780-59e2-9d76-b4af35790d12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dM}{dt}=k(50-M)" :db/id 17592186306166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cc9a611-32b3-5c07-adf4-0f5db91760e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dM}{dt}=k(60-M)" :db/id 17592186305416} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7bbd6e9-b260-5c66-af8a-149b7483e375" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186308101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e2af3a7-6f0f-5f5a-a5bb-60da7750b37e" :question/math-academy-id "q-335145" :question/problem "A marine rehabilitation aquarium initially houses $25$ marine animals. The number of animals in the aquarium $M(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in months. If the maximum capacity of the aquarium is $60$ animals, which differential equation could be used to model the animal population? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Since the number of animals in the aquarium obeys an inhibited growth model, the rate at which the number of animals changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the number of animals in the aquarium. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-M),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the aquarium, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=60$ animals, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=k(60-M).\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186317472 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53776591-13cc-549e-9f32-c5abaeced1dc" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Growth"}] :question/answer-fields 