ked-solution "The rate at which the frog population $F(t)$ of the wetland changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum frog population the wetland can support and the current frog population. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-F),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum frog population the wetland can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=100\\text{ frogs},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=k(100-F).\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186317473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53776591-13cc-549e-9f32-c5abaeced1dc" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Growth"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0259fd14-c9a9-58b6-a404-bff6dbf7badd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=k(500-S)" :db/id 17592186315128} {:answer/id #uuid "2b505a49-68dc-5562-bdf8-5c978ed54f78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=kS(500-S)" :db/id 17592186315372} {:answer/id #uuid "71a08b4a-f491-5881-ac62-23a6895de4b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=k{S}^{2}(500-S)" :db/id 17592186315763} {:answer/id #uuid "8331eb98-12ad-5b45-8ce6-0bb6ebdd5d83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-k(500-S)" :db/id 17592186315845} {:answer/id #uuid "cf5b4005-e626-5fa7-abfe-28e258dd834a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-k(500+S)" :db/id 17592186316288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0259fd14-c9a9-58b6-a404-bff6dbf7badd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=k(500-S)" :db/id 17592186315128} :answer-field/id #uuid "43fa6037-cb1e-50be-a0f9-38bac3fc52f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186316700}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab916584-a26d-54f0-907f-592aaa033fea" :question/math-academy-id "q-344218" :question/problem "A school administrator observed that the growth of the number of students enrolled in a program is directly proportional to the difference between the program’s maximum enrollment capacity and the current number of students enrolled. Let $S(t)$ be the number of students enrolled, measured in students, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum number of students the program can support is $500$ students, which differential equation could be used to model the number of students enrolled in the program? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of students enrolled $S(t)$ in the program changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum number of students the program can support and the current number of students enrolled. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-S),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum number of students the program can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=500\\text{ students},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=k(500-S).\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186317474 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53776591-13cc-549e-9f32-c5abaeced1dc" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Growth"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f087d09-e1e7-5ab8-982f-cbc3540e7119" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(240-P)" :db/id 17592186315209} {:answer/id #uuid "bfbca73a-2595-5694-b56c-f975654c4d33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=kP(240-P)" :db/id 17592186316199} {:answer/id #uuid "d2316d69-8166-551c-a84b-3d2353b842a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=-k(240-P)" :db/id 17592186316296} {:answer/id #uuid "df79cf0d-10f8-59a7-8669-02f7ce1dd947" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k{P}^{2}(240-P)" :db/id 17592186316354} {:answer/id #uuid "e28e46f1-8fff-5681-9163-603d3ff9e8da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=-k(240+P)" :db/id 17592186316374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f087d09-e1e7-5ab8-982f-cbc3540e7119" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(240-P)" :db/id 17592186315209} :answer-field/id #uuid "64f84fa9-a2f0-5ca4-b07d-e3fc3dc62fae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186316786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "54e7291f-5b5b-5bf3-a3dd-9eac24a3f4e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-344219" :question/problem "An engineer observed that the growth of the number of solar panels installed at a facility is directly proportional to the difference between the facility’s maximum number of solar panels and the current number installed. Let $P(t)$ be the number of solar panels, measured in panels, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum number of solar panels the facility can support is $240$ panels, which differential equation could be used to model the number of solar panels installed? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of solar panels $P(t)$ installed changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum number of solar panels the facility can support and the current number of solar panels. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum number of solar panels the facility can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=240\\text{ panels},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(240-P).\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186317477 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53776591-13cc-549e-9f32-c5abaeced1dc" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Growth"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a6efe89-1d82-5203-8a6a-24c18502b095" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(80-B)" :db/id 17592186315360} {:answer/id #uuid "56b622c6-6784-5363-8e24-c96bb51d0957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(35-B)" :db/id 17592186315619} {:answer/id #uuid "8251af8d-948e-5507-b3a9-8ad30939c31a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(25-B)" :db/id 17592186315838} {:answer/id #uuid "996235ec-ab0f-5b92-84a3-56a94cd4f991" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(50-B)" :db/id 17592186315975} {:answer/id #uuid "f38c1553-bd69-57f7-ac0c-a7f412f84db1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(18-B)" :db/id 17592186316478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "996235ec-ab0f-5b92-84a3-56a94cd4f991" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(50-B)" :db/id 17592186315975} :answer-field/id #uuid "f05a2153-5b63-5b4c-9cfe-238a0120df42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186317118}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "999dd29f-4bf1-52fd-b7b1-1ef128e903af" :question/math-academy-id "q-344228" :question/problem "A laboratory incubator initially contains $18$ bacterial cultures. The number of cultures in the incubator $B(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in days. If the maximum capacity of the incubator is $50$ cultures, which differential equation could be used to model the number of cultures? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Since the number of cultures in the incubator obeys an inhibited growth model, the rate at which the number of cultures changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the number of cultures in the incubator. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-B),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the incubator, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=50$ cultures, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=k(50-B).\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186317478 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53776591-13cc-549e-9f32-c5abaeced1dc" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Growth"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11749536-a0f7-5d13-b256-6fdeb5b98399" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dC}{dt}=k(320-C)" :db/id 17592186315223} {:answer/id #uuid "151ae25d-d64b-5851-adfb-806b30416fbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dC}{dt}=k(800-C)" :db/id 17592186315244} {:answer/id #uuid "5a620149-4b03-5b37-bbf4-472c6b574ef2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dC}{dt}=k(1000-C)" :db/id 17592186315631} {:answer/id #uuid "66e6db5c-1814-50aa-8ce2-db2edeec9f31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dC}{dt}=k(500-C)" :db/id 17592186315692} {:answer/id #uuid "c799b39e-df7d-5d1d-bea3-881572cd85f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dC}{dt}=k(1500-C)" :db/id 17592186316245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a620149-4b03-5b37-bbf4-472c6b574ef2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dC}{dt}=k(1000-C)" :db/id 17592186315631} :answer-field/id #uuid "393ad745-9bcc-5868-a810-b633bf129cee" :answer-field/key "selec