ledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Growth"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "043dc448-2d64-5bd5-b613-9351f25fcbe7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt}=k(120-V)" :db/id 17592186445331} {:answer/id #uuid "a61a1a66-20a6-5535-8610-2d133482e2d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt}=k(300-V)" :db/id 17592186445357} {:answer/id #uuid "b18a7bfd-9662-5019-81d6-5d34da173873" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt}=k(500-V)" :db/id 17592186445358} {:answer/id #uuid "bb01bcbc-7723-5c54-bf42-c8d5e8f3b897" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt}=k(70-V)" :db/id 17592186445360} {:answer/id #uuid "d0d96262-4712-58ed-b608-ce9c6dab0eee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt}=k(250-V)" :db/id 17592186445365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0d96262-4712-58ed-b608-ce9c6dab0eee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt}=k(250-V)" :db/id 17592186445365} :answer-field/id #uuid "d06901e9-40fe-5fc7-ae69-f932c626e4ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186445381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4108ce6a-5d28-540e-b8c0-e4b52161a458" :question/math-academy-id "q-344232" :question/problem "A parking garage initially contains $70$ vehicles. The number of vehicles in the garage $V(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in hours. If the maximum capacity of the garage is $250$ vehicles, which differential equation could be used to model the number of vehicles in the garage? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Since the number of vehicles in the garage obeys an inhibited growth model, the rate at which the number of vehicles changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the number of vehicles in the garage. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-V),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the garage, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=250$ vehicles, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k(250-V).\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186445385 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53776591-13cc-549e-9f32-c5abaeced1dc" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Growth"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10c45811-628c-5ba4-8e0a-f7d902d884a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(110-B)" :db/id 17592186445336} {:answer/id #uuid "207c4468-291f-59e8-87bb-de8bc6fba242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(30-B)" :db/id 17592186445338} {:answer/id #uuid "353017b2-4607-5104-afdb-932463c44124" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(280-B)" :db/id 17592186445341} {:answer/id #uuid "83fdb903-a322-5109-85eb-f06a871c051d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(190-B)" :db/id 17592186445350} {:answer/id #uuid "c25a8b30-27a2-57da-864d-b562f2ea668b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(70-B)" :db/id 17592186445361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10c45811-628c-5ba4-8e0a-f7d902d884a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(110-B)" :db/id 17592186445336} :answer-field/id #uuid "49c71e39-1e77-56e6-92ec-6a20e0c6c4b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186445379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "531996ff-0ee7-5943-af5b-93c7fc2f947e" :question/math-academy-id "q-335144" :question/problem "A bird sanctuary initially has $80$ birds. The number of birds in the sanctuary $B(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in months. If the maximum capacity of the sanctuary is $110$ birds, which differential equation could be used to model the bird population? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Since the number of birds in the sanctuary obeys an inhibited growth model, the rate at which the number of birds changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the number of birds in the sanctuary. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-B),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the bird sanctuary, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=110$ birds, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=k(110-B).\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186486384 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53776591-13cc-549e-9f32-c5abaeced1dc" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Growth"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04278896-0d0b-5abb-8627-033b3e542c42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-k(75+H)" :db/id 17592186485774} {:answer/id #uuid "09aa1eb1-e4d1-5ba6-831f-ff5b8460e95e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=k{H}^{2}(75-H)" :db/id 17592186485785} {:answer/id #uuid "37ece5ef-defd-5270-8c15-ef93957859b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=kH(75-H)" :db/id 17592186485872} {:answer/id #uuid "5245158e-ddf4-52ef-a4a6-05b22e789e91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=k(75-H)" :db/id 17592186485914} {:answer/id #uuid "6c0c4047-f0a5-5bbe-9e6d-196692c6e874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-k(75-H)" :db/id 17592186485950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5245158e-ddf4-52ef-a4a6-05b22e789e91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=k(75-H)" :db/id 17592186485914} :answer-field/id #uuid "15815c67-c7dd-5da5-a708-0978463c7ec0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186486244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "56f2ee29-f876-5b21-80e6-83404415a207" :question/math-academy-id "q-344215" :question/problem "A hospital administrator observed that the growth of the number of occupied beds in a hospital is directly proportional to the difference between the hospital’s maximum bed capacity and the current number of occupied beds. Let $H(t)$ be the number of occupied beds, measured in beds, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum number of beds the hospital can support is $75$ beds, which differential equation could be used to model the number of occupied beds in the hospital? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of occupied beds $H(t)$ in the hospital changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum number of beds the hospital can support and the current number of occupied beds. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-H),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum number of beds the hospital can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=75\\text{ beds},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=k(75-H).\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Growth"}
